// One block, decoded — and the precondition that a type already in `core` removes.
//
// The last entry to be merged in `../../../QUESTIONS.md` split 45 *checked elsewhere* comments into
// 40 invariants and **5 preconditions**, and said the five wanted something the list did not have.
// They do not. All five were read, and this file is the two that are the same shape.
//
// `packages/zstd/src/block.wac`:
//
//     export void decodeCompressed(u8[] src, i32 at, i32 size, Buf out, Decoder d) {
//       // The caller has already checked the block fits in the frame, so `end` is within `src`.
//       i32 end = at + size;
//
// and `packages/zstd/src/sequences.wac`:
//
//     // Bounded by the caller, which has already checked the block fits in the frame.
//     i32 end = at + len;
//
// Three arguments where the invariant is `at + size <= src.len()`, asserted in a comment, twice.

import { Result, Bytes } from "core";
import { Buf } from "@/packages/bytes";
import { Fault, Corrupt } from "./stream.wac";

/**
 * One block's bytes, and nothing else.
 *
 * **The precondition is the type**, and the type is `Bytes` — `core/slice.wac`'s `Slice<u8>`, which
 * carries `from` and `len` and whose `slice(lo, hi)` traps on a range it does not contain. A caller
 * that has checked the block fits in the frame expresses that by *making the slice*; one that has
 * not cannot make it without tripping the trap where the mistake is, rather than three calls later.
 * The comment has nowhere left to go.
 *
 * No feature is missing. `Slice` is in `core` today and `packages/bytes` had none when this code was
 * written — the same reason `@/packages/ssh/src/knownhosts.wac` walks a comma-separated field with
 * two cursors instead of splitting into views.
 */
export Result<void, Fault> decodeCompressed(Bytes block, Buf out, Decoder d) { … }

/** Sequences, over the same slice discipline. */
export Result<SeqTables, Fault> decodeAndExecute(
  Bytes block, Bytes literals, Buf out, const SeqTables previous, const Reps reps) { … }

export struct Decoder { … }
export struct SeqTables { … }
export struct Reps { … }

// ## 1. All five preconditions were read, and none wants a feature that is not already listed
//
// | | what it says | what answers it |
// |---|---|---|
// | `zstd/src/block.wac` | *"The caller has already checked the block fits in the frame"* | `Slice`, in `core` today |
// | `zstd/src/sequences.wac` | *"Bounded by the caller, which has already checked …"* | the same |
// | `ssh/src/wire.wac` | *"a caller can parse a whole message and check once at the end"* | `try` |
// | `tls/src/asn1.wac` | *"the same check written once per call site and forgotten at one of them"* | `try` |
// | `wacc/src/wapyparse.wac` | *"The caller has already checked that the word after `async` is `def`"* | a witness value — the private-constructor entry |
//
// So the branch that entry opened closes: **two want a type that exists, two want a construct
// already proposed, and one is the entry it was written beside.** The interesting part is the middle
// pair, because they are the strongest argument *against* the construct this directory has proposed
// eight times.
//
// ## 2. The two latches are the best case against `try`, and they make it
//
// `@/packages/rlp` replaced a sticky-error cursor with `try` and argued that a dropped check becomes
// a type error rather than a discipline. `ssh/src/wire.wac` and `tls/src/asn1.wac` both keep the
// latch and both say why, and their reasons are the two halves of one objection:
//
// > `ok` latches false on the first read that would run past the end, and every later read is a
// > no-op returning zero. That means a caller can parse a whole message and **check once at the
// > end**, instead of testing after every field — which is how a missed check turns into a read of
// > whatever happened to follow in memory.
//
// > It is shared rather than per-cursor because `element` hands out a cursor over a nested region,
// > and a failure two levels down has to be visible to the caller that started the parse —
// > otherwise each level would have to check and pass it up, which is **the same check written once
// > per call site and forgotten at one of them**.
//
// Both are describing the cost of *manual* propagation, which is what `try` removes: the per-field
// test is one token, and forgetting it at one call site is the compile error the second quote is
// afraid of. So the objection is to a `Result` **without** `try`, and neither file could have known
// that, because `try` is this directory's proposal and their latches predate it.
//
// What survives it is smaller and real: a latch answers *harmless defaults* after a failure, so a
// parser keeps running and produces a whole malformed structure that a caller inspects once. `try`
// stops at the first fault. For a certificate parser that difference is a design choice about
// whether you want the first error or the shape of the input, and `@/packages/tls`'s is the one
// package here that might genuinely want the second.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`Decoder` and `SeqTables` carry state across blocks and the type does not say which frame.**
// A `SeqTables` from one frame handed to another frame's block decodes garbage — the shipped
// signature threads them through explicitly, which is right, and nothing stops the wrong one being
// threaded. That was filed as the phantom-parameter ask at an eighth site and **it is not one** —
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s count of eleven was enumerated on 2026-09-05 and three are a larger
// feature. This is one of the three, and this file said why at the time without drawing the
// conclusion: the first
// where the tag would be a *runtime* value (a frame) rather than a static one.
//
// **A block's size comes from its header and bounds the slice that contains the header.** The
// three-byte block header says how long the block is, so the slice cannot be made until it is read,
// and reading it needs a slice. The shipped code resolves this by passing `src` and offsets and
// checking; here it is `Bytes.slice(lo, hi)` on the frame, which traps.
//
// **This said *a slice that refuses is the missing piece* and that was wrong**, checked the next
// day. Of the eight `slice` call sites in this directory, three take input-derived bounds — this,
// `@/packages/abi`'s `blobAt` and `@/packages/rlp`'s `take` — and **all three check first and
// produce a fault carrying numbers**: `LengthOverruns(at, n, end - at - 32)`,
// `Truncated(this.at, n, this.src.len() - this.at)`. A nullable `take(lo, hi)` answers `null`, which
// cannot say how far over the length ran, so it would replace a good diagnostic with a worse one and
// leave the check where it is.
//
// So `core/slice.wac` is right and its stated reason was incomplete rather than wrong: a trapping
// bound is correct when the caller **knows** the bounds *or* **wants to describe** why they were
// wrong, and only a caller content with *no* wants the nullable form. There is not one here.
// `core/slice.wac` now says both.
